COMMAND NAME: mppinitstandby.sh

Adds and/or initializes a standby master host for a Greenplum Database system.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppinitstandby.sh -s <standby_hostname> [-i] [-n] [-a] [-q] 
[-l <logfile_directory>] [-D] [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppinitstandby.sh script will create a backup master host if one 
has not already been created for your Greenplum Database system. Before 
running this script, make sure that the Greenplum Database software is 
installed on the backup master host and that you have exchanged SSH 
keys between hosts. This script should be run on the currently active 
primary master host. 

The script will perform the following steps:

* Shutdown your Greenplum Database system

* Update the Greenplum Database system catalog to add the new backup master 
  host information (use the -n option to skip this step)

* Setup the backup master instance on the alternate master host

* Start the synchronization process

* Restart your Greenplum Database system

A backup master host serves as a warm standby in the event of the 
primary master host becoming unoperational. The backup master is kept 
up to date by a transaction log replication process (gpsyncmaster), 
which runs on the backup master host and keeps the data between the 
primary and backup master hosts synchronized. If the primary master fails, 
the log replication process is shutdown, and the backup master can be 
activated in its place by using the mppactivatestandby.sh script. Upon 
activation of the backup master, the replicated logs are used to 
reconstruct the state of the master host at the time of the last 
successfully committed transaction.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-s <standby_hostname>

Required. The host name of the standby master host.


-i (do not start synchronization)

This will not start the synchronization process (gpsyncmaster) between the 
backup and primary master hosts. The default is to start the 
synchronization process.


-n (do not update catalogs)

Use this option if you already have a standby master configured, and 
just want to resynchronize the data between the primary and backup master 
host. The Greenplum system catalog tables will not be updated.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is 
still written to the log file.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add a backup master host to your Greenplum Database system and start the 
synchronization process:

mppinitstandby.sh -s host09


Start an existing backup master host and synchronize the data with the primary master 
host - do not add a new Greenplum backup master host to the system catalog:

mppinitstandby.sh -s host09 -n


